# ── Endpoint ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which flavour of console this is. Usually inferred, so you rarely set it:
#   unifios  UDM / UDM-Pro / UDM-SE / UDR / UCG / UX / Cloud Key Gen2+ /
#            UniFi OS Server. Serves the official Integration API. (default)
#   cloud    reached through api.ui.com; works behind CGNAT, valid certificate.
#            Inferred when UNIFI_CONSOLE_ID is set.
#   classic  self-hosted Java Network application on port 8443. Has NO
#            Integration API at all — legacy transport only. Inferred from :8443.
# Synonyms are accepted, because local-versus-cloud is the distinction people
# are actually making: `local`, `console` and `unifi-os` all mean unifios;
# `remote` means cloud; `self-hosted` means classic. An unrecognised value is
# reported and ignored rather than being fatal.
# UNIFI_MODE=unifios

# The console. A pasted browser URL works — the scheme, port and path are split
# out — so all of these are equivalent inputs:
#   192.168.1.1   https://192.168.1.1   https://192.168.1.1/network/default/…
UNIFI_HOST=192.168.1.1

# Only when it is not the default for the mode (443 for unifios, 8443 classic).
# UNIFI_PORT=8443

# Cloud mode only: the console id from its URL at https://unifi.ui.com.
# UNIFI_CONSOLE_ID=

# ── Auth: the Integration API (primary) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Two DIFFERENT kinds of key exist and they are not interchangeable:
#
#   (a) LOCAL Integration key — for UNIFI_MODE=unifios. Created on the console:
#         https://<console>/network/default/settings/control-plane/integrations
#       Use the URL; on Network 10.6 `Control Plane` is under a heading named
#       after the console at the BOTTOM of the settings sidebar, below `System`,
#       which is why people conclude it is missing.
#
#   (b) CLOUD Site Manager key — for UNIFI_MODE=cloud, with UNIFI_CONSOLE_ID:
#         https://unifi.ui.com/settings/api-keys
#       A console's LOCAL API rejects this key with a 401. Cloud mode works
#       behind CGNAT and needs no TLS workaround, since api.ui.com has a real
#       certificate. List your consoles and their ids with:
#         curl -H "X-API-KEY: $KEY" https://api.ui.com/v1/hosts
#
# Either kind is displayed once and can afterwards only be renamed or deleted.
# Any admin can create one. Creation sometimes errors on the first attempt;
# retry. The key inherits the role of the admin who created it, so a read-only
# admin's key cannot drive the write tools.
UNIFI_API_KEY=

# ── Auth: the legacy controller API (optional second tier) ───────────────────
# Off by default. Enabling it registers the unifi_legacy_* tools, which cover
# what the official API does not: blocking and reconnecting clients, events,
# alarms, health, port forwarding, adoption and firmware upgrades.
#
# This is a cookie session, NOT the API key — it needs a real console account.
# Use a dedicated LOCAL admin: a Ubiquiti SSO account requires MFA, which cannot
# be scripted. The session is held in memory only and never written to disk.
# UNIFI_ENABLE_LEGACY=1
# UNIFI_USERNAME=
# UNIFI_PASSWORD=

# ── Defaults ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The site every tool uses when none is given. Accepts the site UUID, its
# internalReference (the legacy 8-character name, usually "default") or its
# display name — all three are resolved. Only needed on a multi-site console:
# with exactly one site, that site is used automatically.
# UNIFI_SITE=default

# ── Safety ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Off by default. When off, the mutating tools are NOT REGISTERED — they do not
# appear in tools/list and cannot be called at all, rather than being refused.
#
# Turning this on adds: restart a device, power-cycle a PoE port, authorize and
# unauthorize guests, create and delete vouchers, and — with the legacy tier —
# block, unblock and reconnect clients. Firewall and network configuration stay
# read-only either way.
# UNIFI_ALLOW_WRITES=1

# ── TLS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Local consoles ship a SELF-SIGNED certificate, so a plain connection fails.
#
# Verifying needs TWO things together, and either alone achieves nothing:
#
#   1. Trust the certificate, because it is self-signed:
#        openssl s_client -showcerts -connect 192.168.1.1:443 </dev/null \
#          2>/dev/null | openssl x509 > ~/.config/unifi/console.pem
#        export NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS=~/.config/unifi/console.pem
#   2. Address the console BY NAME. The certificate is issued to `unifi.local`
#      with SANs for unifi.local, localhost and 127.0.0.1 — and NO IP SAN — so
#      UNIFI_HOST=192.168.1.1 fails on the host name no matter how the
#      certificate is trusted. (Measured: `curl --cacert` gives ssl_verify=0 by
#      name, ssl_verify=1 by IP.) A hosts entry is enough to provide the name.
#
# The alternatives, in order of preference:
#
#   - UNIFI_MODE=cloud with UNIFI_CONSOLE_ID — api.ui.com has a real
#     certificate, so the problem disappears entirely.
#   - Last resort: disable verification. Scoped to THIS server's requests via a
#     dedicated undici dispatcher — it is not NODE_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED and
#     does not affect anything else in the process. It is refused in cloud mode,
#     where it would be a pure downgrade. The startup banner prints
#     tls=INSECURE whenever it is on.
# UNIFI_INSECURE_TLS=1

# ── Optional tuning ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Pin the console's Network version instead of probing it at startup. The tool
# list depends on this version (7 endpoints on 9.0, 44 on 10.3+), so setting it
# skips a network round trip and makes the tool list deterministic. Read it from
# GET /v1/info, or from unifi_get_console_info.
# UNIFI_APP_VERSION=10.6.101

# UNIFI_MAX_RETRIES=3           # retries on 429 and 5xx
# UNIFI_PAGE_LIMIT=50           # default page size (the API caps it at 200)
# UNIFI_MAX_PAGES=20            # hard bound on pagination per tool call
# UNIFI_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS=3000   # startup probe budget; failure is non-fatal
# UNIFI_CONFIG=~/.config/unifi/config.json   # JSON alternative to these vars
# UNIFI_DEBUG=1                 # verbose request logging, on stderr
